1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,930
Good Sunday evening, and welcome to our weekly look
back at your garden.

2
00:00:05,230 --> 00:00:09,370
In this autumn week the garden was once again full
of life and song.

3
00:00:09,670 --> 00:00:14,320
Almost three thousand eight hundred times the little
chiffchaff called from the shrubs.

4
00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:19,110
The coal tit, too, was heard a good two thousand six
hundred times in the garden.

5
00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:24,550
In all, fifty-one different bird species could be
identified this week.

6
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:30,300
The most frequent callers were the chiffchaff, the
coal tit, the great spotted woodpecker and the
magpie.

7
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:35,200
On Saturday a very special guest appeared at your
garden pond.

8
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:39,730
Five times the heavy mute swan with its white
plumage could be heard.

9
00:00:40,030 --> 00:00:46,440
This largest native waterbird reaches a wingspan of
up to two hundred and forty centimetres.

10
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:50,900
To take off, it first runs powerfully across the
water for a while.

11
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:55,580
Only on Thursday was the call of a fieldfare heard,
just once.

12
00:00:55,880 --> 00:00:59,580
The fieldfare is only slightly smaller than the
better-known blackbird.

13
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:04,160
On its autumn passage it often calls in flight, a
harsh chattering.

14
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:09,510
On their way to south-western Europe these birds
rest sociably on meadows.

15
00:01:09,810 --> 00:01:14,720
The rarest guest, on Sunday, was a black-headed
gull, heard a single time.

16
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:19,660
This gull got its name because its call recalls
mocking laughter.

17
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:25,350
With a body length of at most thirty-nine
centimetres it is rather dainty.

18
00:01:25,650 --> 00:01:29,510
God keep you, and until next Sunday evening here
with us.
